# November 21, 2026: a second scatter of places across Iowa, which had 17 on the map (region ia claimed with
# tools/choose.py --thinnest). Spread over every corner: the lakes and the Missouri in the northwest, the Nishnabotna
# and Tabor in the southwest, Des Moines and Pella in the middle, Eldon, Ottumwa, Keosauqua, Lamoni and Davis City in
# the south, the Amanas, Stone City, Anamosa and Cedar Rapids in the east, Decorah and Nashua in the northeast, Clear
# Lake in the north. Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text and Register list rows (tools/wiki_raw.py);
# the managing bodies' own pages read with tools/page.py (State Historical Society of Iowa, westbendgrotto.com,
# hitchcockhouse.org, the Tabor Historical Society, salisburyhouse.org, pellahistorical.org, americangothichouse.org,
# villagesofvanburen.com, cofchrist.org, amanaheritage.org, doc.iowa.gov, the City of Decorah, littlebrownchurch.org,
# brucemore.org, surfballroom.com) and the National Park Service's page for the Floyd monument.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'ia_bdef','label':'Iowa: a second scatter','added':'2026-11-21 03:14'}
PLACES=[
# ---- Northwest: the lakes and the Missouri ----
N('abbie-gardner-sharp-cabin','Abbie Gardner Sharp Cabin (Spirit Lake Massacre Log Cabin)','Arnolds Park','ia','landmark','standing',43.36559,-95.14064,'site','open','drive','','1856–',
  'The log cabin of the Gardner family, settlers on the shore of West Okoboji Lake, who were killed in March 1857 in the attack on the frontier settlements known as the Spirit Lake Massacre; thirteen-year-old Abbie Gardner was taken captive and released 84 days later. She bought the cabin back in 1891 and ran it as one of Iowa\'s first tourist attractions, selling her book and postcards there, until her death in 1921. The state bought it in 1941 and passed it to the State Historical Society of Iowa in 1974.',
  'The restored cabin, put back to its 1856 appearance under architects and archaeologists, beside the memorial site in Arnolds Park. Admission is free (interior otherwise not checked).',
  'The cabin a captive girl bought back and turned into a roadside attraction.',
  'State Historical Society of Iowa site at 74 Monument Drive, Arnolds Park; group tours through the Dickinson County Conservation Board. Hours from the Society\'s page; closed from Labor Day to Memorial Day. Street parking in Arnolds Park (no lot of its own found on the map).',
  [W('Spirit Lake Massacre'),L('State Historical Society of Iowa: Abbie Gardner Sharp Cabin','https://history.iowa.gov/history/sites/abbie-gardner-sharp-cabin')],
  brick='no',
  hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day: Tuesday to Sunday noon to 4 p.m.; closed the rest of the year'),
N('grotto-of-the-redemption','Shrine of the Grotto of the Redemption','West Bend','ia','worship','standing',42.96385,-94.44614,'exact','open','drive','','1912–1954',
  'Nine grottos showing scenes in the life of Christ, built from rocks, minerals, shells and petrified wood set in concrete by Father Paul Dobberstein, a German-born priest who promised a shrine after recovering from pneumonia. He began in 1912 and kept on, year round, for 42 years, joined by Father Louis Greving from 1946; by his death in 1954 it covered a city block. It is believed to be the largest grotto of its kind, and was put on the National Register in 2001.',
  'The grottos themselves, a museum of precious and semiprecious stones and of the shrine\'s building, a gift shop and a campground. Mass at Ss. Peter and Paul beside it on Thursday at 5:30 p.m. and Sunday at 10 a.m.',
  'A priest\'s forty-two years of rocks and gems pressed into concrete.',
  'The Shrine of the Grotto of the Redemption, 208 1st Avenue NW, West Bend (Diocese of Sioux City). The shrine\'s page says it is open 24 hours a day; guided tours start at the museum. Gift shop November to April 30, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., May to October 9 to 5, closed in January. Parking on the streets round the shrine (no lot found on the map).',
  [W('Grotto of the Redemption'),L('The Shrine of the Grotto of the Redemption','https://westbendgrotto.com/')],
  brick='no',
  hours='Shrine open 24 hours; guided tours April to October daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('sergeant-floyd-monument','Sergeant Floyd Monument','Sioux City','ia','landmark','standing',42.46266,-96.37800,'exact','public','drive','','1901',
  'A 100-foot obelisk of white sandstone on Floyd\'s Bluff above the Missouri, over the grave of Sergeant Charles Floyd, the one member of the Lewis and Clark Expedition to die on the journey, on August 20, 1804, most likely of a burst appendix. Erosion had carried much of the first grave into the river by 1857; his remains were reburied in 1895 and for the last time in 1900, and the obelisk was finished on May 30, 1901. It was the first place named a National Historic Landmark in the United States.',
  'The obelisk on the bluff, with benches, interpretive panels and a view of the Missouri valley.',
  'The first National Historic Landmark: a sergeant\'s grave on a bluff.',
  'City of Sioux City land at 2601 South Lewis Boulevard; the National Park Service page names the Sioux City Museum as manager and lists auto parking. The lot is just southwest of the obelisk.',
  [W('Sergeant Floyd Monument'),L('National Park Service: Sergeant Floyd Monument','https://www.nps.gov/places/sergeant-floyd-monument.htm')],
  th=[42.46250,-96.37704],thn='Lot on South Lewis Boulevard',
  brick='no'),
# ---- Southwest: the Nishnabotna and Tabor ----
N('hitchcock-house-lewis','Reverend George B. Hitchcock House','Lewis','ia','landmark','standing',41.30325,-95.10347,'site','open','drive','','1856–',
  'A two-storey house of local sandstone with walls 21 inches thick, built about 1855 by the Congregationalist minister George B. Hitchcock on a hill above the East Nishnabotna River, overlooking two east-west trails to the river ferries. Hitchcock lived there from 1856 to 1865 and is documented as sheltering and moving people escaping slavery; the house became a National Historic Landmark in 2006 and is on the National Park Service\'s Network to Freedom.',
  'A vernacular Federal house of random ashlar sandstone on a rubblestone foundation, hand-hewn timbers inside. In the basement, a room thought purpose-built as a hiding place, once reached through a false cabinet, and a second room with a cooking fireplace and an outside door; visitors enter the basement by that door. The house says much of it is original. Photographs allowed, no video.',
  'A stone house on the Underground Railroad, with a hidden room in the cellar.',
  'Museum run by the Hitchcock House, west of the Lewis city limits. Guided tours from its own page; closed on Mother\'s Day, Father\'s Day and July 4. Parking at the house (no mapped lot; not checked on the aerial).',
  [W('Reverend George B. Hitchcock House'),L('The Rev. George B. Hitchcock House','https://hitchcockhouse.org/')],
  brick='no',
  hours='May 2 to September 15: Thursday to Sunday, guided tours from 1 p.m., last tour 4:15 p.m.'),
N('todd-house-tabor','Todd House','Tabor','ia','landmark','standing',40.89944,-95.67639,'site','open','drive','','1853–',
  'The home of the abolitionist Congregationalist minister John Todd and his wife Martha, finished in the summer of 1853 as the third building in the new village of Tabor, which Todd helped found along with Tabor College. John Brown visited, and the house was a stop on the Underground Railroad before the Civil War. Todd was a model for the grandfather in the novel Gilead.',
  'A two-storey frame house with clapboards. The Tabor Historical Society says it was built of adobe brick footings, logs and milled timber with cottonwood siding, sashes and doors of black walnut by the cabinetmaker Samuel Adams, and a stone foundation put in about 1858 (interior not checked).',
  'The Tabor parsonage where John Brown came calling.',
  'Museum of the Tabor Historical Society at 705 Park Street, Tabor; tours by appointment by telephone (the number is on the society\'s page). Street parking.',
  [W('Todd House (Tabor, Iowa)'),L('Tabor Historical Society: Todd House Museum','https://www.taboriowahistoricalsociety.org/todd-house-museum')],
  brick='no',
  hours='Tours by appointment'),
# ---- The middle: Des Moines and Pella ----
N('salisbury-house-des-moines','Salisbury House','Des Moines','ia','landmark','standing',41.57941,-93.67146,'exact','open','drive','','1923–1928',
  'A manor house in Tudor, Gothic and Carolean styles on a wooded hill, built from 1923 to 1928 by the cosmetics maker Carl Weeks and his wife Edith Van Slyke Weeks. Most of the family\'s collections of art, rare books, musical instruments, documents and arms are still in the house. It is owned and run by the Salisbury House Foundation.',
  'The house and its collections, shown on self-guided tours whenever the museum is open (last entry an hour before closing) and on guided tours of about 75 minutes for up to ten; the gardens and grounds are free whenever the office or museum is open. The museum closes November 23 to December 8, 2026 for holiday decorating.',
  'A cosmetics fortune\'s English manor on a Des Moines hill.',
  'Salisbury House Foundation, 4025 Tonawanda Drive, Des Moines; admission to the museum. Accessible parking and a little ordinary parking on the north side of the property. Hours as the foundation\'s visit page gave them for the week of November 16, 2026.',
  [W('Salisbury House (Des Moines, Iowa)'),L('Salisbury House: Visit','https://salisburyhouse.org/visit/'),L('Salisbury House: Museum information','https://salisburyhouse.org/visit/museum-information/')],
  brick='?',
  hours='Wednesday to Sunday noon to 5 p.m.; closed Monday and Tuesday'),
N('scholte-house-pella','Dominie Henry P. Scholte House','Pella','ia','landmark','standing',41.40862,-92.91697,'site','open','walk','','1847–',
  'The house Hendrik (Henry) Scholte, leader of the Dutch settlers who founded Pella, built for his wife Maria in six months in 1847 and 1848, on what is now the north side of Pella\'s square. It grew from a few rooms to some 26 to 30, and the family lived in it for generations. It is a museum of Pella Historical, with a tea room and gardens.',
  'The library is the room most as it was in the Scholtes\' day; the rest was changed as the family came and went, and the Rose Room was the carriage house. Guided by the museum; the gardens are free and always open.',
  'The founder\'s house on the square of a Dutch prairie town.',
  'Pella Historical, 728 Franklin Street, Pella; admission to the house (hours and prices differ during Tulip Time). Its own lot is just east of the house, entered from Washington Street; free street parking across from it and on Franklin Street.',
  [L('Pella Historical: Scholte House','https://www.pellahistorical.org/scholtehouse'),L('Pella Historical: Visit','https://www.pellahistorical.org/visit')],
  brick='no',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 1 to 4 p.m.; closed Sunday and Monday; gardens always open'),
# ---- The south: Eldon, Ottumwa, Keosauqua, Lamoni, Davis City ----
N('american-gothic-house','American Gothic House (Dibble House)','Eldon','ia','landmark','standing',40.92168,-92.21327,'exact','public','drive','','1881–',
  'A small Carpenter Gothic house with a pointed upper window, built in 1881 and 1882 by Charles and Catharine Dibble, which Grant Wood used as the backdrop of his 1930 painting American Gothic after seeing it only twice. It stayed a private home well into the twentieth century; after thirty years of preservation work it was given to the State Historical Society of Iowa in 1991. It is kept as it looked in 1930.',
  'A 504-square-foot white house with board-and-batten siding and the Gothic window, seen from the grounds and the visitor centre beside it, which tells the story of Wood and the house. The house itself is opened only for prearranged group tours of ten or more, and then only the ground floor.',
  'The house behind the farmer and his pitchfork.',
  'The house belongs to the State Historical Society of Iowa; the American Gothic House Center next door is run separately and says so. The house is seen from outside; the Center is open year-round except holidays. The mapped lot is just south of the house, on American Gothic Trail.',
  [W('American Gothic House'),L('State Historical Society of Iowa: American Gothic House','https://history.iowa.gov/history/sites/american-gothic-house'),L('American Gothic House Center: hours','https://americangothichouse.org/visit')],
  th=[40.92082,-92.21350],thn='Lot on American Gothic Trail',
  brick='no',
  hours='Visitor centre Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday 1 to 4 p.m.; house by group tour only'),
N('mars-hill-ottumwa','Mars Hill log church and cemetery','Ottumwa','ia','worship','standing',40.89945,-92.35751,'site','view','drive','','1850–1857',
  'A Baptist church of dovetail-notched hewn logs built between 1850 and 1857 on land given by Barbara Clark, with its cemetery, south of Ottumwa. Local lore says it was a stop on the Underground Railroad. An arsonist nearly destroyed it in 2006; it was rebuilt and rededicated on June 8, 2008, with some of the charred original logs still in it. Wikipedia calls it one of the largest log buildings and one of the oldest log churches still in use in Iowa.',
  'A rectangular hewn-log church with a gable roof (the front door is a copy of the original) and its burying ground. A service is held once a year, in June; interior not checked.',
  'A hewn-log church that came back from an arsonist\'s fire.',
  'Owner not checked; the church is now interdenominational and holds a yearly service in June (Wikipedia). Look from the road unless it is open. No mapped parking; the pin is the Register coordinate and was not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Mars Hill (Iowa)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('hotel-manning-keosauqua','Hotel Manning','Keosauqua','ia','hotel','standing',40.72981,-91.96119,'exact','open','drive','','1897–',
  'A riverside hotel in Steamboat Gothic style, built from 1897 to 1899 by Edwin Manning, one of Keosauqua\'s founders, on the walls and foundation left by two fires in the 1890s in his store and bank; he added the second and third storeys. It opened on April 27, 1899. The Des Moines River has flooded it at least four times, worst in 1901, seven feet deep. It was put on the National Register in 1973.',
  'The hotel on the bank of the Des Moines River, restored to look much as it did about 1900, its lobby and rooms furnished with period antiques. Villages of Van Buren calls it a community-owned boutique hotel.',
  'A hotel built like a riverboat, on a river that kept coming in.',
  'Working hotel at Van Buren and Cass streets on the river in Keosauqua; guests and diners go in. The county tourism page lists it as community-owned. Opening hours for non-guests not found; small lots on Park and Cass streets beside it.',
  [W('Hotel Manning'),L('Villages of Van Buren: Hotel Manning','https://villagesofvanburen.com/stay/hotel_manning/')],
  th=[40.73022,-91.96099],thn='Lot on Park Street',
  brick='yes',
  hours='?'),
N('liberty-hall-lamoni','Liberty Hall (Joseph Smith III house)','Lamoni','ia','landmark','standing',40.62417,-93.95222,'site','open','drive','','1881–',
  'A vernacular Victorian house built in 1881 by the carpenter Thomas Jacobs for Joseph Smith III, president of the Reorganized Church of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints, when the church had its headquarters in Lamoni. Smith left in 1906 when the headquarters moved to Independence, Missouri. It was then a church home for the aged, part of a church dairy farm, a Civilian Conservation Corps office from 1934 to 1941, and a home for the aged again. The church, now Community of Christ, restored it from 1973 and shows it as a museum.',
  'The house restored to its appearance in Smith\'s time; interior not checked beyond that.',
  'The prophet\'s house in a prairie church town.',
  'Community of Christ historic site in Lamoni. Hours from the church\'s page for May to December 2026; tours outside them by arrangement by telephone. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Liberty Hall (Lamoni, Iowa)'),L('Community of Christ: Lamoni, Iowa','https://cofchrist.org/lamoni-iowa-usa')],
  brick='?',
  hours='May to December: Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Sunday by appointment; closed Monday'),
N('union-church-davis-city','Union Church (Brick Church)','Davis City','ia','worship','standing',40.63833,-93.80611,'site','view','drive','','1878',
  'A brick church built in 1878 at the urging of John Clark, a Scot of no particular denomination, because Davis City had gone twenty years without a church. It was open to every denomination and is owned jointly by Methodist, Presbyterian and Christian congregations. It was put on the National Register in 1976.',
  'A 36 by 56 foot brick building with a clock and bell tower on the front, the bell hung outside the tower; entry doors with fanlights either side of the tower and round-arched windows. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned jointly by three congregations (Wikipedia); no page of its own was found, so look from the street. Street parking; not checked.',
  [W('Union Church (Davis City, Iowa)')],
  brick='yes'),
# ---- The east: the Amanas, Stone City, Anamosa, Cedar Rapids ----
N('amana-colonies','Amana Colonies','Amana','ia','town','standing',41.80009,-91.87312,'area','public','walk','','1855–1932',
  'Seven villages on 26,000 acres (Amana, East, High, Middle, South and West Amana, and Homestead) built by the German Radical Pietists of the True Inspiration Congregations, who came from near Buffalo, New York, in the 1850s and lived communally for eighty years, until 1932. Each village had a church, school, bakery, dairy, wine cellar, post office, sawmill and store, and fifty-five communal kitchens fed the colonists. The colonies became a National Historic Landmark in 1965.',
  'Village streets of two-storey houses, most of a local sandstone of an unusual colour, and the communal buildings. The Amana Heritage Society runs the Amana Heritage Museum and other sites, among them the Communal Kitchen and Cooper Shop museum, the Homestead church museum and the High Amana General Store. The pin is the Heritage Museum in Amana.',
  'Eighty years of a communal village life, in sandstone.',
  'The villages are public streets with private houses, shops and restaurants. The Amana Heritage Museum is at 705 44th Avenue, Amana; the society\'s page loads its hours by script and could not be read beyond a winter-festival day (10 a.m. to 4 p.m.). Public lots in Amana south of 44th Avenue (the lot point OpenStreetMap gives on 44th Avenue fell on houses and was not used).',
  [W('Amana Colonies'),L('Amana Heritage Society','https://amanaheritage.org/')],
  brick='?'),
N('stone-city-iowa','Stone City','Stone City','ia','town','standing',42.115113,-91.354691,'site','view','drive','','1850–',
  'A company town on the Wapsipinicon River for the workers of the Anamosa limestone quarries, begun in 1850. From 1859 to 1895 it shipped stone for railroad bridges, piers and foundations, and by 1880 had five hundred people, the three-storey stone Columbia Hall hotel and opera house of 1883, and John Green\'s twelve-room mansion. Grant Wood painted it in 1930 and ran the Stone City Art Colony on the Green estate in 1932. Columbia Hall was torn down in 1938 for its stone; the mansion burned in 1963 and was pulled down in the 1990s.',
  'Limestone buildings of the quarry town in the river valley: St. Joseph\'s Catholic Church of 1913–1914, built of donated quarry stone with stained glass from Germany (an oratory since the parish closed in 1992), the schoolhouse, the general store, the blacksmith shop, the Dearborn house, and a stone barn of 1889 and a stone water tower that Wikipedia pictures. The church, store and blacksmith shop are in Wood\'s painting.',
  'Grant Wood\'s painted village, built of its own quarries.',
  'An unincorporated community; most buildings are private homes, seen from the roads. The Stone City Foundation\'s site would not open, so whether St. Joseph\'s opens to visitors was not checked. The nearest mapped lot is about a kilometre west on Stone City Road at the Matsell Bridge Natural Area.',
  [W('Stone City, Iowa'),W("St. Joseph's Roman Catholic Church (Stone City, Iowa)")],
  brick='no'),
N('anamosa-state-penitentiary','Anamosa State Penitentiary','Anamosa','ia','inst','standing',42.11144,-91.28946,'site','view','drive','','1872–',
  'A state prison in Scottish Baronial Revival style, its outer walls and crenellated towers built like a medieval fortress of stone from its own quarry, beginning in 1873 with twenty prisoners brought from Fort Madison; it opened in 1874. The architects were William Foster and Henry Liebbe. It is still a working medium and maximum security prison.',
  'The fortress front and the administration building, its entrance guarded by stone lions, seen from the street. The Anamosa State Penitentiary Museum is just outside the walls in a stone building that was a prison barn and then a cheese factory; Wikipedia says it opens seasonally and has a gift shop.',
  'A working prison built to look like a castle.',
  'Iowa Department of Corrections, 406 North High Street, Anamosa. An active prison: look from the street only. The museum\'s own site would not open (404), so its hours were not checked. Parking on Jackson and North Garnavillo streets.',
  [W('Anamosa State Penitentiary'),L('Iowa Department of Corrections: Anamosa State Penitentiary','https://doc.iowa.gov/find-facility/asp')],
  brick='no'),
N('brucemore-cedar-rapids','Brucemore','Cedar Rapids','ia','inst','standing',41.99240,-91.63940,'exact','open','drive','','1884–1886',
  'A Queen Anne mansion of three storeys and twenty-one rooms on a 26-acre estate, built from 1884 to 1886 by Caroline Sinclair, widow of the meatpacker Thomas Sinclair, and later home to the families of George Bruce Douglas (starch) and Howard Hall (manufacturing). Margaret Hall gave it to the National Trust for Historic Preservation; it is Iowa\'s only National Trust Historic Site, run with Brucemore, Inc.',
  'The mansion with its steep gables, five chimneys and turrets, shown on guided tours; formal gardens, a children\'s garden, a night garden, pond, orchard and woodland, free to walk.',
  'A meatpacker\'s widow\'s mansion that became Iowa\'s National Trust site.',
  'Brucemore, Inc., 2160 Linden Drive SE, Cedar Rapids; tickets for mansion tours (typically not on Monday or Tuesday; see its calendar). Parking on site near the carriage house and mansion. Hours from its visitor page, November 2026.',
  [W('Brucemore'),L('Brucemore: Visitor information','https://www.brucemore.org/visitor-information/')],
  brick='?',
  hours='Gardens Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 4 p.m., Saturday 10:30 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday 1 to 4 p.m.; mansion tours by calendar, not Monday or Tuesday'),
# ---- The northeast: Decorah and Nashua ----
N('painter-bernatz-mill','Painter-Bernatz Mill','Decorah','ia','industry','standing',43.30583,-91.79222,'site','open','walk','','1851–1964',
  'A stone flour mill begun by William Painter in 1851 and enlarged to its present size within a year or two, the first of at least fifteen mills on the Upper Iowa River and the oldest building in Decorah. A race from a dam on the river ran beneath it to the turbine; diesel replaced water in 1947 and it milled until 1964. It belongs to the Vesterheim Norwegian-American Museum: given in 1968 according to the City of Decorah, part of the museum from 1971 according to Wikipedia.',
  'The lower two storeys of native limestone; the hip roof was replaced between 1874 and 1890 by the present gable roof with its big loft. It holds the museum\'s exhibits on farming and industry (interior otherwise not checked).',
  'Decorah\'s oldest building, a stone mill over its own race.',
  'Part of Vesterheim, the National Norwegian-American Museum, in downtown Decorah; admission to the museum. Vesterheim\'s pages refused the reading tool (HTTP 403), so hours were not checked. Lots west of the mill on 5th Avenue and east downtown on Claiborne Drive.',
  [W('Painter-Bernatz Mill'),L('City of Decorah: Painter-Bernatz Mill','https://decorahia.org/commission-and-boards/historic-preservation/decorah-historic-sites/painter-bernatz-mill')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',
  hours='?'),
N('little-brown-church-nashua','The Little Brown Church in the Vale','Nashua','ia','worship','standing',42.96197,-92.50818,'exact','open','drive','','1864–',
  'The church of the song "The Church in the Wildwood", which Dr. William Pitts wrote in 1857 after a coach stop at Bradford and taught to his singing class for the church\'s dedication in 1864, finding on his return in 1862 a church going up where he had pictured it. It closed in 1888 when Bradford declined; a society formed to keep it, and services began again by 1914. Thousands come each year to see it or to be married in it. It belongs to the National Association of Congregational Christian Churches.',
  'The small church in its valley, where every Sunday service still sings "The Church in the Wildwood" and rings the bell on the third verse. Weddings and vow renewals Thursday to Saturday all year (interior not checked beyond that).',
  'The church a song pictured before it was built.',
  'Active congregation at 2730 Cheyenne Avenue, near Nashua. The church\'s page says it is open to visitors daily. The mapped lot is beside the church on Cheyenne Avenue.',
  [W('The Little Brown Church'),L('The Little Brown Church in the Vale','https://littlebrownchurch.org/')],
  th=[42.96230,-92.50838],thn='Lot on Cheyenne Avenue',
  brick='?',
  hours='Open to visitors daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday service 10:30 a.m.'),
# ---- The north: Clear Lake ----
N('surf-ballroom-clear-lake','Surf Ballroom','Clear Lake','ia','amuse','standing',43.13969,-93.38980,'exact','open','drive','','1948–',
  'The dance hall where Buddy Holly, Ritchie Valens and J. P. "The Big Bopper" Richardson gave their last show on February 2, 1959, the night before the plane crash known as "The Day the Music Died". The first Surf opened on April 17, 1933 and burned on April 20, 1947; it was rebuilt across the street and reopened on July 1, 1948. National Register 2011, National Historic Landmark 2021.',
  'The ballroom still in use, with its South Seas fittings: murals of waves, boats and palm trees, bamboo and rattan furniture, fake palms beside the stage and clouds painted on the ceiling, a 6,300-square-foot dance floor, a music museum and a Wall of Fame. Parts may be shut for concerts and private hire.',
  'The stage of the last show before the music died.',
  'Surf Ballroom & Museum, 460 North Shore Drive, Clear Lake; open year-round, hours from its own page. Free parking in the large lot on the north side.',
  [W('Surf Ballroom'),L('Surf Ballroom: Hours of operation','https://surfballroom.com/?p=341903')],
  th=[43.14013,-93.38922],thn='Surf Ballroom lot, north side',
  brick='?',
  hours='Wednesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Sunday 11 a.m. to 3 p.m. Memorial Day to Labor Day'),
]
